&lt;p&gt;# Comments: 0&lt;/p&gt;</description><guid isPermaLink="false">https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50285918</guid><pubDate>Thu, 29 Oct 2026 08:17:08 +0000</pubDate></item><item><title>Ask HN: Why not AI assisted ZFS rewrite?</title><link>https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50285879</link><description>&lt;p&gt;Just thinking out loud..&lt;p&gt;If folks are doing a clean room rewrite of Adobe {Photoshop, et al}, MS Office, Fusion CAD etc, why don’t someone who works on filesystems do a clean room rewrite of ZFS and upstream it to the Linux kernel? 
BcacheFS was exciting until the core dev fought his way out of the Linux kernel. ZFS works great but has no legal pathway into the Linux kernel. But, Now that LLMs can largely write code faster under the watchful guidance of experienced engineers who knows what to do, why not wield that power to create something that is &lt;i&gt;like&lt;/i&gt; ZFS but not encumbered by Oracle and have it part of Linux kernel?&lt;p&gt;I know the usual caveats about file systems, needing to be mature, battle tested and that vibe coding file systems is not the greatest of ideas.. if _I_ were to unilaterally vibe code a ZFS with LLMs and tell folks to use it, it will be colossally stupid. I mean it as a wide open source project with participation from the right people with an open mind.&lt;/p&gt;
